# tools/s/scale_area_witness.rish -- the area-average resample proves itself on metal (Season G).
#
# Season G (Open Media Primitives) -- the DECIMATION sibling of the nearest-neighbor and bilinear resizes
# (tools/s/scale_witness.rish, tools/s/scale_bilinear_witness.rish). Where nearest copies the one nearest source pixel whole
# and bilinear reads the four pixels around each sample, area averaging reads EVERY source pixel a destination cell covers
# and takes their coverage-weighted mean -- so shrinking a photo far below half size keeps every source pixel's
# contribution rather than dropping the pixels between the taps, the clean downscale a thumbnail wants where nearest and
# bilinear would alias.
#
# The crux this witness proves is that area averaging is an exact integer box MEAN, not a float or a lucky sample: a
# destination cell's coverage of each source pixel is the integer overlap of two subunit spans, so a partial edge pixel
# carries its true fractional share and a resize is deterministic byte-for-byte. On metal: an identity resize reproduces
# the source byte-for-byte; a 2x2 pattern shrunk to 1x1 takes the true average of all four corners rather than picking one;
# a 4x4 black/white checker shrunk to 1x1 reads a true mid-gray rather than aliasing to a single extreme (the anti-aliasing
# claim plain); a solid image decimates to the same solid color; a 3px-to-2px downscale splits the shared middle pixel
# fractionally between the two outputs; alpha averages like any other channel; the resized image round-trips through the
# open QOI codec byte-for-byte; a zero dimension, an empty source, and an over-max target refuse by name having allocated
# nothing; and the same resize is deterministic. No network, no key, no funds -- the clean downscale a surface runs the day
# it wants a photo shrunk without moire, on our own open geometry.
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/s/scale_area_witness.rish

say "scale-area: Language EN -- Season G geometry; an RGBA image resampled to a new size by area averaging, the decimation sibling of the nearest and bilinear resizes, the verb a downscale reaches for where nearest and bilinear would alias."
say "scale-area: Style Radiant -- exact integer coverage, not a float box: one per-axis coverage map seen twice, each covered source pixel weighted by its integer subunit overlap, every output the box sum over a constant total weight with half-up rounding, so a partial edge pixel carries its true share and a resize is deterministic."
say "scale-area: Lens TAME -- u32 in-memory counts, u64 for the subunit products and the box accumulator so neither can wrap, u8 output channels proven a real byte; every buffer names a maximum (qoi_max_dim - qoi_max_pixels); each function states its invariants positively; the whole target bound-checked once up front so a refusal allocates nothing; integer only so a resize is deterministic; no network, no key, no funds."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "image/bin/scale_area"

let build = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p image/bin && env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build image/scale_area.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "scale-area: build failed -- ${build.err}"

let selftest = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert selftest.ok else "scale-area: selftest exited non-zero -- ${selftest.out}"

let out = trim selftest.out
say "scale-area: ${out}"

assert selftest.out contains "GREEN image-scale-area" else "scale-area: selftest did not print the GREEN line"

say "scale-area: GREEN -- the clean downscale shrinks a picture without aliasing, proven on metal beside the nearest and bilinear resizes."
